== 1. inverse-kinematics conditioning ==
  frozen nominal  thigh 0.800: singular values 0.4222 / 0.3910 / 0.1380 m/rad, condition number 3.060
    worst joint rate per 1 m/s lateral foot motion 2.7366 rad/s
  aligned crouch  thigh 0.731: singular values 0.4258 / 0.3867 / 0.1384 m/rad, condition number 3.076
    worst joint rate per 1 m/s lateral foot motion 2.7366 rad/s
  the aligned crouch is 4 degrees from the frozen one, so conditioning barely moves: no new singularity risk

== 2. is the 60 mm landing cap a workspace limit? ==
  FL foot can move  163.6 mm outward +y at constant height inside the compiled joint limits
  FL foot can move  306.2 mm inward -y at constant height inside the compiled joint limits
  FL foot can move  263.9 mm forward +x at constant height inside the compiled joint limits
  FL foot can move  278.4 mm back -x at constant height inside the compiled joint limits
  smallest reach 163.6 mm against the 60 mm landing cap: the cap uses 36.7% of the workspace, so it is a design choice, not an IK limit

== 3. stance-leg torque while carrying its share ==
  four-wheel support, mg/4 per wheel: 118.1 N -> joint torques (+20.865, -0.040, -23.789) N*m, worst 23.789 N*m, 29.7% of 80.0 N*m
  two-support diagonal, mg/2 per wheel: 236.2 N -> joint torques (+37.728, -0.895, -44.359) N*m, worst 44.359 N*m, 55.4% of 80.0 N*m
  a pure stance PD of kp=80.0 could not hold this at all: 0.554 rad of droop. The original force allocation feed-forward is what holds the stance leg, and the saved run droops only 0.0211 rad, so the feed-forward is carrying essentially all of it. This is inherited behaviour, not a new risk.
  rejecting the optimised gait's 103 N swing reaction adds 51.5 N lateral per stance wheel:
    static hold alone 44.359 N*m -> with rejection 56.544 N*m, 70.7% of the limit
    torque headroom left 23.46 N*m (29.3%), so the actuator is not the limit on rejecting it - whether the PD loops actually do is a separate, simulation-only question

== 4. holding the aligned crouch against real tracking error ==
  statics tolerates thigh error +/-0.0554 rad (3.17 deg)
  the saved C35 pre-state sat 0.0211 rad below its 0.800 command, which is 38% of that tolerance
  so the aligned crouch must be defined on the ACTUAL pose, not the command: commanding 0.7313 and settling 0.0211 low lands at 0.7102 rad, still inside the window but 62% of margin left
  commanding 0.7524 instead recentres it; the new contract should specify the aligned crouch as a measured-pose target with a bias for the observed droop

== 5. the entry cost nobody has priced: the feet are planted ==
  relative to the base, the aligned crouch puts each wheel +25.53 mm in x compared with the frozen nominal
  base height is preserved: 0.452366 -> 0.452366 m
  but the wheels are ON THE GROUND, so changing the leg angles moves the BASE -25.53 mm in x instead, unless the wheels roll
  that is 85% of the frozen 30 mm longitudinal excursion limit, leaving only 4.5 mm for the rest of the task
  alternative: roll the wheels 0.2934 rad (16.81 deg) while morphing the legs, so the contact pattern moves and the base stays put, spending no excursion budget at all
    the wheels are actuated (12 N*m) and the contract already replaces the wheel PD during side control, so this is a reference change, not a new authority; it does require releasing the 12-position wheel brake for the morph
  the morph itself is small: thigh 0.0687 rad, calf 0.0023 rad, so at the 4.0 rad/s reference clip it needs only 0.0172 s, far inside the 200-control preparation window

== 6. can the hip roll axis alone do the lateral motion? ==
  first: the hip roll axis IS already the primary lateral actuator
    hip   +2.7366 rad/s per 1 m/s of lateral foot speed, 44.5% of the total joint rate
    thigh -1.1239 rad/s per 1 m/s of lateral foot speed, 18.3% of the total joint rate
    calf  +2.2901 rad/s per 1 m/s of lateral foot speed, 37.2% of the total joint rate
  but hip rotation alone sweeps the foot on an arc, so it cannot hold height:
    hip-to-wheel radius 0.4019 m
     hip (rad)   dy (mm)   dz (mm)
        0.1000      35.8      16.1
        0.2000      69.7      35.7
        0.4000     131.0      84.5
        0.6000     181.4     144.5
        0.7854     216.5     208.0
    even 0.1 rad already lifts the foot 16 mm while moving it 36 mm sideways, so thigh and calf must coordinate - which is exactly what the paired IK does
  second, and this is the blocking one: the wheel axle is along body y, so the
    wheel rolls only along x. Any lateral motion of its CONTACT POINT is pure
    sliding. Translating the base sideways with all four wheels planted therefore
    requires all four contacts to slide, and with no gripping contact left there
    is no anchor to push against: internal joint motion produces no net lateral
    translation. Lateral travel fundamentally requires unloading wheels so they
    can be repositioned, which is the stepping gait. The 12 mm clearance gate exists to make that repositioning a clean non-sliding motion.
  third, the useful part of the question: the step is capped at 60 mm, which is
    only 36.7% of the 163.6 mm workspace, so a bigger lateral move per step is kinematically available. What actually caps it is two-support statics at about 77.5 mm; raising the cap to that limit is the legitimate version of "take a bigger lateral step".

== 7. verdict ==
  none of the four checks blocks the aligned crouch:
    conditioning barely changes, the landing cap uses a third of the workspace, stance torque keeps headroom even while rejecting the swing reaction, and the morph takes 0.02 s
  one real finding: if the base moves, the crouch change spends 25.53 mm, 85% of the 30 mm longitudinal excursion budget, so the new contract must roll the wheels through the morph or raise that limit
  and one specification requirement: define the aligned crouch on the measured pose with a droop bias, not on the commanded angle
  still not addressed here: whether the body and attitude PD loops actually reject the 22% of body weight swing reaction, which needs simulation, and anything off flat ground
